---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import Photo from '../../components/Photo.astro';
import { locationsHubPhoto } from '../../data/photos';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { towns } from '../../data/towns';
import { pagedNeighborhoods } from '../../lib/geo';
import { writtenTowns, mentionOnlyTowns } from '../../lib/geo-content';
import { business, isReady } from '../../data/business';

const path = '/locations/';
const skagitOk = isReady(business.territory.skagitConfirmed)
  ? business.territory.skagitConfirmed === true
  : false;

const built = await writtenTowns();
const whatcom = built.filter((t) => t.county === 'Whatcom County');
const skagit = built.filter((t) => t.county === 'Skagit County');
/* Now includes towns that earn a page and have not had one written yet, so
   dropping them from the rails does not quietly shrink the service area. */
const mentionOnly = await mentionOnlyTowns();

/* Every neighborhood that HAS a page links from here — via pagedNeighborhoods,
   never a local filter. The old /service-areas/ page linked only the 31 city
   pages, orphaning the whole neighborhood tier; and this rail is the third
   place that enumerated neighborhoods independently and produced dead links
   when the capacity gate went in. One source of truth, everywhere. */
const answer =
  'Sasquatch Pest Control serves Bellingham and the surrounding Whatcom County communities, including Ferndale, Lynden, Blaine, Birch Bay, Everson, Nooksack, Sumas and Sudden Valley. Find your city below for local pest pressure, the neighborhoods we cover, and how to reach a crew near you.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which cities does Sasquatch Pest Control serve?',
    a: 'Bellingham, Ferndale, Lynden, Blaine, Birch Bay, Everson, Nooksack, Sumas, Sudden Valley and the surrounding unincorporated communities in Whatcom County.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know if my address is in your service area?',
    a: 'Find your city on this page and open its page for the neighborhoods we cover. If you do not see your community listed, call or text us and we will tell you directly whether we run that route.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you charge extra for outlying communities?',
    a: 'Travel is built into our normal pricing across the areas listed on this page. If an address sits outside the routes we run, we will say so before scheduling rather than adding a surcharge later.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  path={path}
  title="Pest Control Service Areas | Whatcom County, WA"
  description="Sasquatch Pest Control serves Bellingham, Ferndale, Lynden, Blaine, Birch Bay and the surrounding Whatcom County communities. Find your city and neighborhood."
  kind="page"
  faqs={faqs}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Service areas', path }]}
>
  <h1>Pest Control Service Areas</h1>
  <AnswerBox text={answer} />

  {/* THE LEAD FORM SITS HERE, HIGH, and the position is the point.
      It used to hang off the foot of Cta.astro, which put it under three
      thousand words: a reader who had already decided to act had to scroll
      an entire article to find anything to act on. The quick answer still
      leads — it is one short paragraph and the schema's speakable selector
      points at it — and the form comes straight after.
  
      ONE PER PAGE. The GHL loader binds to its iframe by id, so a second
      copy would break both; the CTA at the foot no longer carries one and
      keeps its phone button. The sticky mobile bar jumps to this. */}
  <LeadForm />

  {/* The hub is not a place, so it gets the vehicle rather than a town. */}
  <Photo photo={locationsHubPhoto} />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>One county, several different pest problems</h2>
    <p>
      Whatcom County is small enough to drive across in under an hour and varied
      enough that the work changes completely depending on where you are standing.
      We run four distinctly different kinds of job here, and which one you get
      depends almost entirely on your address.
    </p>

    <h3>The city, and its two housing stocks</h3>
    <p>
      Bellingham is really two building stocks sharing a map. The older
      neighborhoods — Lettered Streets, Columbia, Sunnyland, York, parts of
      Fairhaven and South Hill — are largely prewar, with post-and-pier
      foundations, crawlspaces that were never properly sealed, and framing from
      an era when nobody thought about air-sealing a penetration. Those houses get
      rodents, carpenter ants and moisture ants, and the entry points are usually
      structural rather than behavioral.
    </p>
    <p>
      The newer hillside and edge neighborhoods — Alabama Hill, Barkley,
      Cordata, King Mountain — are a different problem. Cedar siding, vented
      crawlspaces, soffit venting that a rat treats as a front door, and mature
      landscaping held tight against the foundation. Here the fix is more often
      exclusion and perimeter work than interior treatment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then there is the student rental belt around Sehome, Happy Valley and the
      Western campus, where high tenant turnover drives a bed bug pattern you do
      not see elsewhere in the county.
    </p>

    <h3>The agricultural corridor</h3>
    <p>
      North and east of the city — Lynden, Everson, Nooksack, Sumas and the farm
      country between them — the work is agricultural. Rodents in feed rooms,
      outbuildings and equipment sheds, fly pressure around livestock, and
      cluster flies overwintering in south-facing walls by the thousand. This is
      exclusion and sanitation work more than treatment, and it runs on
      the farm's calendar
      rather than ours.
    </p>

    <h3>The waterfront</h3>
    <p>
      Blaine, Birch Bay, Semiahmoo and the Bellingham waterfront
      share a marina rodent problem, salt exposure on every building, and a large stock of seasonal
      and second homes that sit empty for months at a time. An unoccupied house
      through a wet Whatcom winter is an invitation, and a good share of our
      spring calls out there are for something that moved in during December.
    </p>

    <h3>The lake and forest edge</h3>
    <p>
      Sudden Valley, Silver Beach, Edgemoor and the Lake Whatcom shoreline bring
      1970s cedar construction against a working forest edge. Carpenter ants in
      the cedar, rodents pushing in from the treeline, and damp crawlspaces — which come with their own timing rules, because exclusion cannot
      happen during the maternity season.
    </p>

    <h2>Where we do and don't go</h2>
    <p>
      We build our routes around Whatcom County and we say so plainly. If your
      address sits outside the areas listed on this page, we will tell you before
      scheduling rather than adding a travel surcharge afterwards, and where
      another operator covers that ground better we will say that too.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not service Point Roberts at this time. It is Whatcom County on
      paper, but the only road to it runs through Canada and back, and that is
      not a route we run. If you are there, we would rather say so now than take
      the booking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not every community on this page has its own page. Where we do not have
      enough that is genuinely specific to say about a place, we would rather list
      it honestly under "also serving" than publish a page that is the same words
      with the town name swapped.
    </p>

    <h2>How quickly we can get to you</h2>
    <p>
      One to two days is the normal wait anywhere in Whatcom County, and about
      three across August and September, which are our heaviest weeks and the
      point at which everybody phones at once. Bellingham and the immediate ring — Ferndale, Sudden Valley,
      the Guide Meridian corridor — sit at the short end of that and are often
      sooner for an active problem. Lynden, Everson, Blaine and Birch Bay run on
      scheduled route days, so which day you land on depends on where the
      address falls in the week rather than on how long the wait is. Skagit
      addresses — Mount Vernon, Burlington, Sedro-Woolley, Bow and Alger — are
      route days too, and we will tell you which one when you call.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wasp and hornet work in late summer is the one thing we routinely prioritize
      out of order, because a nest near a doorway or a child's play area is a
      different kind of urgent than an ant trail in a kitchen.
    </p>

    <h2>Renting, or managing a rental?</h2>
    <p>
      Well over half of Bellingham households rent, which makes this worth
      spelling out. Bellingham Municipal Code 6.15 requires rental properties to
      pass a certified inspection, and the checklist explicitly flags foundation
      openings larger than a quarter inch as an infestation risk. Washington's
      landlord-tenant law, RCW 59.18.060, places responsibility for control on the
      landlord in multi-unit buildings, with a specific carve-out for
      single-family homes.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice that means the answer to "who pays for this" depends on the
      building type and on how the infestation started, and it is worth knowing
      before the conversation rather than during it. We work with tenants, owners
      and property managers, and we will tell you plainly which of those we think
      needs to be in the room.
    </p>
    <h2>Exclusion across the county</h2>
    <p>
      Exclusion is the service where geography changes the job most, and it is
      worth calling out separately. On the waterfront, salt and wind corrode vent
      screens and fasteners, so anything installed there has to be specified for
      the exposure or it fails in three winters. In the agricultural corridor the
      pressure is constant from surrounding open ground, so the goal is a
      defended building rather than a cleared property. In the older city
      neighborhoods it is post-and-pier foundations and framing cavities that run
      uninterrupted from the crawl to the attic.
    </p>
    <p>
      What does not change is the timing. Late summer is the right window
      everywhere in this county — dry crawlspaces, dry ground, and the autumn
      rodent movement still weeks away. A house sealed in August does not have
      the interior problem. A house sealed in December has an interior problem
      plus whatever is already sealed inside it.
    </p>

    <h2>Second homes and seasonal properties</h2>
    <p>
      Birch Bay, Semiahmoo and the Lake Whatcom shoreline carry a
      lot of housing that sits empty for months. An unoccupied house through a wet
      winter is an open invitation: no one is running water in the traps, no one
      notices droppings accumulating, and no one hears the scratching in the wall.
      A good share of our spring calls in those communities are for something that
      moved in during December.
    </p>
    <p>
      For those properties the useful service is a scheduled check rather than a
      reaction. Catching a rodent entry in November is a different job from
      cleaning up an attic in April.
    </p>


  <section>
    <h2>Whatcom County</h2>
    {whatcom.map((t) => (
      <>
        <h3><a href={`/locations/${t.slug}/`}>{t.name}</a></h3>
        {pagedNeighborhoods(t).length ? (
          <ul class="rail">
            {pagedNeighborhoods(t).map((n) => (
              <li><a href={`/locations/${t.slug}/${n.slug}/`}>{n.name}</a></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        ) : null}
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  {skagitOk && skagit.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Skagit County</h2>
      <ul class="rail">
        {skagit.map((t) => <li><a href={`/locations/${t.slug}/`}>{t.name}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}

  {mentionOnly.length > 0 && (
    <section>
      <h2>Also serving</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        We also run routes through {mentionOnly.join(', ')}. These communities do
        not have their own page — call or text us and we will confirm coverage.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <Faq items={faqs} />
  <Cta market="Whatcom County" />
</BaseLayout>
